comments.body:
**Rejected requests are recorded, so a retrying client is locked out indefinitely.**

`now` is pushed before the `<= max` check, so an over-limit attempt still counts toward the window. Any client retrying faster than `max / windowMs` keeps refreshing its own window and never recovers. Reproduced with `max=2, windowMs=300` and a 50ms retry loop — still blocked after 3s (60 windows); a client that stays fully quiet for one window recovers fine.

Secondary effect: while a client is blocked, `recent` grows without bound and the `filter` on line 6 re-scans it every request. At ~5,000 recorded attempts a single `limit()` call measured ~50µs and keeps growing, so rate-limiting an aggressive caller makes the limiter progressively more expensive — the opposite of what it's for.

Recording only admitted requests fixes both, and matches the "at most `max` requests per `windowMs`" contract in the header comment:

```js
const recent = (hits.get(ip) ?? []).filter((t) => t > now - windowMs);
if (recent.length >= max) {
  hits.set(ip, recent);
  return false;
}
recent.push(now);
hits.set(ip, recent);
return true;
```

Coverage only exercises the burst-within-one-window case, which is the path that already works. The untested paths are the ones with the bugs: expiry after `windowMs`, whether a blocked attempt counts against the window, and per-IP isolation.

Worth adding (the second fails today):

```js
test('separate IPs have separate budgets', () => {
  assert.equal(limit('10.0.0.2', 1), true);
  assert.equal(limit('10.0.0.3', 1), true);
});

test('a blocked attempt does not extend the window', async () => {
  assert.equal(limit('10.0.0.4', 1, 100), true);
  assert.equal(limit('10.0.0.4', 1, 100), false);   // blocked, and recorded
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 60));
  assert.equal(limit('10.0.0.4', 1, 100), false);   // still inside window
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 60));
  assert.equal(limit('10.0.0.4', 1, 100), true);    // 120ms since the allowed hit
});
```

Note also that `hits` is module-level state with no reset hook, so tests have to coordinate on distinct IP strings to stay independent. Exporting a `reset()` for tests (or letting callers pass in a store) would make this suite robust as it grows.

